WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.070
In this video, we
demonstrate that it's

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:05.390
possible to analyze cardiac
pulse from regular videos

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:08.100
by extracting the imperceptible
motions of the head caused

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.600
by blood flow.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.760
Recent work has enabled
the extraction of pulse

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.070
from videos based on
color changes in the skin

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:15.900
due to blood circulation.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.382
If you've seen
someone blush, you

00:00:17.382 --> 00:00:18.840
know that pumping
blood to the face

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.670
can produce a color change.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.480
In contrast, our
approach leverages

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.990
is perhaps more
surprising effect.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:27.630
The inflow of blood doesn't
just change the skin's color.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:30.060
It also causes the head to move.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:33.130
This movement is too small to
be visible with the naked eye,

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:35.400
but we can use video
amplification to review it.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:41.000
Believe it or not, we
all move like bobbleheads

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.740
with different motions
at our heart rate,

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:45.460
but at a much smaller
amplitude than this.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.210
Now, you might wonder
what causes the head

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:48.880
to move like this?

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.440
In each cardiac cycle,
the heart's left ventricle

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.950
contracts and injects blood
at high speed to the aorta.

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:57.050
During the cycle,
roughly 12 grams of blood

00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:00.140
flow to the head from the
aorta by the carotid arteries

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:01.980
on either side of the neck.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:05.269
It is this influx of blood that
generates a force on the head.

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:08.330
Due to Newton's third law, the
force of the blood on the head

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:10.890
equals the force of the
head acting on the blood,

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:13.730
causing a reactionary,
cyclical head movement.

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:16.520
To demonstrate this process,
we coded a toy model

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:19.640
using a transparent mannequin
head, where rubber tubes stand

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.620
for simplified arteries.

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:23.960
Instead of pumping blood,
we will pump compressed air

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:25.650
provided by this air tank.

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:28.375
And I can release the
air using this valve.

00:01:28.375 --> 00:01:29.750
Now, watch what
happens as I open

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:31.280
and close the valve
once a second,

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.470
similar to a normal heart rate.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:34.330
Ready?

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:35.640
Here.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.150
This motion is fairly similar
to the amplified motion

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:40.920
of real heads that
we've seen before.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.440
We exploit this
effect to develop

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:44.030
a technique that
can analyze pulse

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:46.700
in regular videos
of a person's head.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.760
Our method takes an input
video of a stationary person

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.040
and returns a one-dimensional
signal corresponding

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:53.660
to the head motions.

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:56.150
From this signal, we can
extract an average pulse rate,

00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:59.720
as well as beat locations
for deeper clinical analysis.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:02.720
We begin by locating the
face using a face detector

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:05.512
and selecting feature
points within the area.

00:02:05.512 --> 00:02:07.220
The feature points
are tracked from frame

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:09.979
to frame of the video using
the Lucas-Kanade tracking

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:11.300
algorithm.

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:13.230
We use the vertical,
or y component,

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:16.710
of each of the feature point
trajectories for our analysis.

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:18.740
Next, we temporally
filter the signals

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:20.900
through a pass band
encompassing a normal pulse

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:23.120
range, while excluding
extraneous motions

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:24.920
like respiration.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:26.870
We decompose the
multi-dimensional motion

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:29.090
of the head described
by the trajectories

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:33.290
into submotions using Principle
Component Analysis, or PCA.

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:36.860
PCA returns the main directions
along which the head moves.

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:39.320
We project the motion of
the head onto each component

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:42.620
and choose the signal with the
clearest dominant frequency.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:46.070
We use the dominant frequency
to obtain an average pulse rate.

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:49.190
Finally, we perform peak
detection on the chosen signal

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:51.500
to obtain beat locations
for further analysis,

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:54.020
such as heart rate variability.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:56.300
We tested our method on
different people varying

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:59.720
in skin tone and gender and were
able to get nearly exact pulse

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:02.180
rates compared to an ECG device.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:04.640
In addition, our method
produced similar beat length

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:07.820
distributions to the ECG,
an exciting result that

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.890
shows that we can capture
more subtle information

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:12.800
about the heart than
just an average rate.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:16.530
Finally, our method is robust
to different views of the head.

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:18.830
We obtained a pulse
from a sleeping newborn,

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:20.770
from the back of
a subject's head,

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:23.800
and even when a person
is wearing a mask.

